$DIR/main.p:20:8: invalid operation: cannot take address of v[0]: it is a copy returned by the Index method of vec.Vec[point]
    x[k] on a container calls its Index method, which returns a copy of the
    element. A copy has no address that would stay valid, and a pointer to the
    element itself would be invalidated as soon as the container grows or
    rehashes and moves its elements.
    Call a method that returns the pointer explicitly, such as vec.Vec.Ptr or
    hash.Map.Ptr. Its documentation says how long the pointer is valid.
    &s[i] on a slice is safe because a slice never moves its elements. A
    container that grows does, so Plutonium makes the pointer an explicit method
    call rather than give it the same syntax as the safe case.
    Ask for the pointer by name:
        p := v.Ptr(i)
        p.count++
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2454" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:21:2: cannot assign to v[0].x: v[0] is a copy returned by the Index method of vec.Vec[point]
    x[k] on a container is a copy that its Index method returns, so assigning to
    a field or element of it would change only the copy. This is the same rule
    as Go's for map elements.
    Read the element, change it, and store it back with x[k] = v, or change it
    in place through a pointer from a method such as Ptr.
    Store the changed element back:
        p := v[i]
        p.x = 1
        v[i] = p
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2455" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:25:2: cannot assign to t["a"]: table has no method SetIndex
    Library containers such as vec.Vec and hash.Map are indexed through methods.
    x[k] calls x.Index(k), x[k] = v calls x.SetIndex(k, v), and v, ok := x[k]
    calls x.IndexOk(k). The container's type does not have the method this use
    of x[k] needs.
    Add the method to the type, or call another of its methods directly.
    Go builds maps and slices into the language. In Plutonium they are library
    types that own their memory, so the index syntax is lent to them through
    methods.
    To support v, ok := x[k], add:
        func (m *Table) IndexOk(k string) (int, bool) {
            ...
        }
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2451" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:11: cannot use comma-ok on t["b"]: table has no method IndexOk
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2451" for more)

$DIR/main.p:29:6: cannot read load()["a"]: method Index of hash.Map[string, int] has a pointer receiver, and load() is not addressable
    The container's indexing method has a pointer receiver, so x[k] needs the
    address of x. Here x is a value that has no address, such as the result of a
    function call.
    Store the container in a variable first, or index through a pointer to it.
    Store it in a variable first:
        m := load()
        v := m["key"]
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2457" for a longer explanation)
